\par\par \b \uc2  Analysts demand disclosure as Korea crisis looms. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sarah Davison \par \uc2 792 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 1997\par 07:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Nov 12 (Reuters) - Analysts are calling for improved disclosure from South Korea on its dollar-denominated debts amid mounting concern over the state of the economy. \par \par \uc2  South Korea, the world's eleventh largest economy in terms of gross domestic product, faces rapidly rising servicing costs on a mountain of U.S.-dollar denominated corporate debt. The won's recent slide against the U.S. dollar has further swollen those debts. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Korea \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again on Wednesday to defend the won from falling through the key 1,000 level against the U.S. dollar. The won was trading at about 988.80 to the dollar at midday. \par \par \uc2  "The problem is disclosure. It's not great," said an analyst who requested anonymity. \par \par \uc2  "Too much of this is conjecture. Everybody knows they have a really bad debt problem, but it's guess work at the moment as to how bad things are," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Economist Intelligence Unit (EIU), considered by some Asian brokers to be the most reliable source of information on South Korean debt, estimates that the country's external debt burden will rise to US$151 billion by the end of 1997 from 1996 levels of US$137 billion. \par \par \uc2  Short-term debt due to mature within one year amounts to US$77 billion of the 1997 total, the EIU said in figures compiled for its fourth-quarter country risk report. \par \par \uc2  This short-term debt figure, the focus of much of the concern surrounding South Korea's economic condition, compares with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Korea's figure of US$67 billion released last week. \par \par \uc2  One broker estimates more than US$30 billion in short-term paper will mature before the end of the year -- but these figures are, once again, unconfirmed. \par \par \uc2  There is, however, mounting evidence that South Korean corporations are being forced to roll over maturing long-term debt in short-term paper at sharply higher rates following downgrades from international rating agencies. \par \par \uc2  Spreads against comparable U.S. treasury bonds have blown out to more than 320 basis points on the Korean Development Bank (KDB) bond due 2006, considered a benchmark for South Korean debt. The spread was about 100 basis points in June. \par \par \uc2  The EIU said nearly all of the external debt was corporate and denominated in U.S. dollars. This implies heavy risk for corporations without a natural hedge against the declining won through exporting revenue denominated in U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  "With the won going through the floor, U.S. dollar repayments will become that much more expensive," a source said. \par \par \uc2  But even the EIU figures are subject to caution. \par \par \uc2  Short-term debt figures depend upon information from the Organisation for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD), of which South Korea is a member. But the longer-term figures are harder to quantify given that South Korea stopped appearing before the World Bank in 1994. \par \par \uc2  One undeniable fact is that South Korea's debt burden has risen sharply over the past couple of years. A recent SBC Warburg report said South Korea's external debt had risen to current levels from US$108 billion in 1995, US$81.5 billion in 1994, US$64 billion in 1993 and US$60 billion in 1992. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of it comes from expansion earlier on in the decade, the government-sponsored expansion to get market share, to beat the Japanese and get themselves out of recession," another analyst said. \par \par \uc2  "And as you're aware, the government in Korea tells companies what to do and tells banks to lend their money and without credit screening they got themselves into a mess." \par \par \uc2  Financial market players also want to know the condition of the Bank of Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves following reports they had dwindled far below official estimates of US$30.5 billion due to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward commitments to defend the won. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the won market was more restrictive than Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was before the baht fell to speculators in July. Heavy forward positions taken by the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the baht devastated reserves and forced the country to seek aid from the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  has admitted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on the won's behalf, but many analysts believe the Bank of Korea has also stepped into the U.S.-dollar denominated, non-deliverable forward (NDF) market, which operates mostly offshore. \par \par \uc2  "The view is they've been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the NDFs," the first analyst said. "And their reserves haven't gone down over the last two months.... \par \par \uc2  "But all of this is very, very hard to determine." \par \par \uc2  -- Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843 6470 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbc02sgh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
