\par\par \b \uc2  IMF/S. Korea-2: Appears To Contradict BOK's Recent Actions \b0\par\par\uc2 196 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 1997\par 11:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (AP-Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the won, which has depreciated more than 7% this year, the country's finance minister said Sunday. \par \par \uc2  'We're not going to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market,' Kyong Shik Kang, the Minister of Finance and Economy said at a press briefing in Hong Kong. 'The won (level) should be decided by the market.' \par \par \uc2  Kang's statement appears to contradict past actions by the Bank of Korea (BOK), the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which had frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the falling won. \par \par \uc2  At the same briefing, another finance ministry official said the ceiling on foreign ownership of listed South Korean stocks would be increased to 26% from 23% this year. \par \par \uc2  'It will be this year but we don't want to give a specific time because it would affect the stock market,' Woo-Suk Kim, director general at the International Finance and Securities division said. \par \par \uc2  Kang is in Hong Kong attending the World Bank and International Monetary Fund meeting. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 21-09-97 \par \par \uc2  0956GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt9l073jz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
