\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-S.Korean won hits low despite  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoo Choon-sik \par \uc2 463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 1997\par 12:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Sept 19 (Reuter) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at a record low on Friday despite repeated dollar sales by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and analysts predicted worse lay in store for the won. \par \par \uc2  "There are no (dollar) sellers, only buyers. Exporters prefer to hold dollars rather than selling at the current level," said a dealer at a U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  On Friday the won closed at 913.80 to the dollar against Thursday's close of 909.60. \par \par \uc2  The won's fall also contributed to a slide in the Seoul stock market. \par \par \uc2  Namuh Rhee, head of research at Dongbang Peregrine Securities, said: "The trade deficit and the dollar's international trends are the main factors moving the won, and for now, the won's bottom is seen at around 930." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Bank of Korea sold about $400 million in the local interbank market on Friday to cushion the won's fall, which they attributed to a rush for import settlements. \par \par \uc2  A large backlog of import deals had built up during a five-day holiday that lasted through Wednesday, they said. \par \par \uc2  Besides the dollar's strength, the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also been pressured by dollar shortages at local financial institutions as a result of this year's string of corporate failures and debt defaults. \par \par \uc2  The won's plunge hammered the Seoul stock market, pushing the Korean Composite Stock Price Index down 1.74 percent or 12.23 points to 689.16. \par \par \uc2  "The won's fall discourages foreign investors in local stocks and this in turn worries local investors," said Park Byung-moon, head of research at LG Securities. \par \par \uc2  "Foreign investors have already been losing interest in Asian markets following the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis in several Asian countries," he said, referring to slide of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Thailand, the Philippines and Malaysia. \par \par \uc2  A weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually leads to improved price competitiveness of the country's exports, but analysts say it takes time for the benefits to flow. \par \par \uc2  "Investors are worried about the lack of evidence that the weakening won really would boost exports," said Park. "Instead, they are concerned that the weakening won will only end up expanding \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses of companies." \par \par \uc2  For the first eight months of this year, South Korea ran up a current account deficit of $11.1 billion, slightly down from a $12.15 billion shortfall a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  Last year, the current account deficit rose to a record $23.72 billion from $8.95 billion in 1995. \par \par \uc2  The won has been depreciating against the U.S. unit for more than a year. It fell 8.2 percent against the dollar last year, and has dropped 7.6 percent so far this year. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011001dt9j029c2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
