\par\par \b \uc2  ASIAN EXOTICS - Regionals back off in slow trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 11 (Reuter) - Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  drifted lower in thin trade on Friday as players loaded up on dollars ahead of the weekend. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was waiting for fresh political developments in Thailand as well as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest foreign reserves data, due on September 15. \par \par \uc2  "People are still bearish on the baht, but they're a bit cautious at the moment because the Thai Prime Minister has a few options," said a senior dealer at a Singapore bank. \par \par \uc2  He said investors would react negatively if Prime Minister Chavalit Yongchaiyudh decided to dissolve parliament and call a snap election. \par \par \uc2  "If he calls for fresh elections, it would be negative for the baht because more money would be wasted," he said. \par \par \uc2  Worries over Chavalit calling a snap election surfaced after public opinion forced him to endorse a new draft constitution. A final vote on the draft will be held on September 27 following a three-day no-confidence debate. \par \par \uc2  The dealer said the baht's weakness should be limited to 35.25 to the dollar in offshore markets, or 35.75 at worst. \par \par \uc2  It was at 34.75/85 at 0500 GMT against 34.45/34.55 late on Thursday. The onshore rate was 35.65/80 against 35.40/35.60. \par \par \uc2  The baht could also face renewed selling if the Bank of Thailand's end-August \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves showed a significant drop from the August 14 level of $27.9 billion, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Foreign minister Thanong Bidaya told parliament last month Thailand's forward swap contracts, taken to defend the baht from a speculative onslaught in mid-May, could deplete reserves by $6-$14 billion over the following year. \par \par \uc2  In Malaysia, the ringgit extended its overnight losses in thin trade as players continued to focus on stock market weakness. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit was at 2.9530/80 to the dollar while the Kuala Lumpur stock index was down 0.03 percent to 833.92 at midday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market wanted to see more action on the measures announced by Finance Minister Anwar Ibrahim on Wednesday to curb Malaysia's current account deficit and growth. \par \par \uc2  Anwar said several multi-billion construction projects, would be delayed to slow growth and reduce imports. \par \par \uc2  "People are looking for more details of the plan. Right now, all eyes are on the October 17 budget (for 1998)," a dealer at a European bank in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  "They will really have to show that they are going to be restrained about growth. Until the budget, I don't see the ringgit recovering very substantially," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah and Singapore dollar tracked the ringgit's decline, but dealers said trade was lacklustre and there were no signs of large flows. \par \par \uc2  "It's much calmer now. It's not like before when once you got hit, the next offer was 40 or 50 points away. You see now a lot more stability in the market," a local bank dealer in Jakarta said. \par \par \uc2  Spot rupiah was at 2,940/47 against an opening 2,950/60. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was at 1.5127/37 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  But the Philippine peso was still haunted by worries that President Fidel Ramos may try to change the constitution to allow him a second term in office. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the peso's decline was being cushioned by exporter dollar sales amid a dearth of fresh news. \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the won on a tight leash between 908.30 and 909.00 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said upward pressure on the dollar would be dampened by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's planned injection of $500 million into the banking system to ease tightening liquidity ahead of the Chusok thanksgiving holidays next week. \par \par \uc2  -- Madhav Reddy in Kuala Lumpur (603-206 2323), Mantik Kusjanto in Jakarta (6221 384-6364), Anuchit Nguyen in Bangkok (662) 652-0642, Kim Myong-Hwan in Seoul (822) 727 5646, Lilian Karunungan in Manila (6320841-8937) and Sonali Desai in Singapore (65-8703090) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011001dt9c02rvt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
