\par\par \b \uc2  S.Korea declares intention to stabilise  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoo Choon-sik \par \uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 1997\par 11:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Aug 27 (Reuter) - Repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past two days has barely contained the won's slide against the dollar, but the finance ministry on Wednesday said it would not allow the won to fall sharply. \par \par \uc2  The statement had little impact on trading. Dealers said the government was more interested in managing the pace of won depreciation than in reversing the downward trend. \par \par \uc2  The won closed Wednesday's trading at 902.80 against the dollar, down from Tuesday's close of 900.50. \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts said the government believed there was more room for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken. The won has fallen about seven percent against the dollar so far this year, on top of an 8.2 percent decline in 1996. \par \par \uc2  "The government may be well aware the downward trend itself cannot be turned around, and today's announcement is seen to be aimed at slowing the speed," said a domestic bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  He said the finance ministry signalled its stance on Monday when it said it would expand the individual limit on foreign stock ownership to 26 percent from the current 23 percent. \par \par \uc2  "If the ministry wanted to reverse the won's direction, it would have taken a more aggressive policy to bring in dollars," he said, adding that government officials had earlier considered raising the limit to 28 percent. \par \par \uc2  A finance ministry official said the won's rate was within an appropriate range and that the government would try to stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The won is believed to be within an appropriate range when taking into account dollar demand and supply, the dollar's trends abroad and the price competitiveness of our exports," Won Bong-hee, director general of the ministry's financial policy division, told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  "In the future the government will keep the won's rate stable by actively countering steep changes on temporary demand and supply factors or speculative factors," Won said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to fall this week and hit a historic low of 909.50 on Tuesday before several Bank of Korea \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed it to a higher close at 900.50. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  poured more than a billion dollars into the interbank market on Monday and Tuesday, during which the won tumbled by 1.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  "The government seems to just want to stop the won's plunge from dealing an additional blow to the business community already hit by corporate troubles," said a Citibank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "The government is indicating that it would no longer tolerate speculation that the dollar would pass 905 won days later and then 910 very soon after that," said another foreign bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  The won has been pressured by the greenback's strength in international markets and most recently was hit by dollar shortages at domestic banks. \par \par \uc2  South Korean merchant banks and other financial institutions have had difficulty securing dollars as foreign lenders have been reluctant to offer loans in the aftermath of this year's string of corporate failures and huge debt defaults. \par \par \uc2  A cheaper won would contribute to boosting the international price competitiveness of South Korean products by making them cheaper even if the won-denominated prices were kept unchanged. \par \par \uc2  But steep depreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would deter foreign investment in domestic equities and industries for fear of losses in terms of the dollar value. \par \par \uc2  Corporate earnings were also expected to be damaged by the won's steep depreciation because companies have to pay more on their dollar-denominated debts. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt8r00ox3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
