\par\par \b \uc2  S.Korea won tumbles despite repeated  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoo Choon-sik \par \uc2 423 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 1997\par 07:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Aug 25 (Reuter) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's won \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit new lows against the dollar on Monday before recovering slightly after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Korea stepped into the market four times to stem the fall. \par \par \uc2  In a highly unusual move, the Bank of Korea announced early in the morning it would sell dollars in the interbank market to ease the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announcement came after the won fell to an all-time low 902.50 against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The won rebounded on the rare statement by the bank, but only temporarily. \par \par \uc2  At the midday break, the won stood at 903.50, after hitting an all-time low of 907.80 earlier in the day. The won closed at 899.50 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  In the early afternoon, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars for 904 won in its fourth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Korea offered to sell dollars at 923 won in six-month forward trade at about mid-morning, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Minutes later, it stepped in again, this time offering to sell dollars at 944 won in one-year forward trade, they said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the won was pressured by strong dollar demand in line with the continued dollar shortages at merchant banks. \par \par \uc2  Local financial institutions have been struggling to secure dollars in the aftermath of this year's string of corporate debt defaults that affected credit ratings of South Korean banks. \par \par \uc2  Stock investors reacted negatively to the won's sharp fall. \par \par \uc2  The composite index, which had opened higher on hopes for a government package to stabilise financial markets, closed for the lunch break barely higher at 743.43, up 0.85 points. \par \par \uc2  The won has been depreciating against the U.S. unit for more than a year. \par \par \uc2  It fell 8.2 percent against the dollar last year, and 6.6 percent so far this year. \par \par \uc2  The won's daily movement is limited to a 2.25 percent trading band. Its limited convertibility makes it less susceptible than fully convertible \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to speculation. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the won was expected to further weaken in the afternoon unless the government announced fresh steps to counter the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's drop. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar demand was so strong that announcement of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  plan hardly affected the trading sentiment," said a foreign bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "Even when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actually stepped in, the impact was limited as traders thought the amount was small when comparing with the strong demand." \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt8p00tnv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
