\par\par \b \uc2  S.Korea won tumbles as  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Robin Bulman \par \uc2 634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 1997\par 11:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Aug 25 (Reuter) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbled to a new low against the U.S. dollar on Monday even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned speculators it would step in to stem the fall -- and then did so five times. \par \par \uc2  Analysts were divided on the reasons for the won's dive. \par \par \uc2  One said it was a "self-fulfilling prophecy" of jittery corporate treasurers. Another blamed an expected widening of South Korea's August trade deficit. \par \par \uc2  But analysts agreed that the won, which is not pegged to the dollar and not fully convertible, was not under a serious speculative attack similar to an assault that has battered Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The won closed on Monday at 904.60 against the dollar, compared to Friday's close of 899.50 won. \par \par \uc2  In a highly unusual move, the Bank of Korea announced early in Monday morning trading it would sell dollars in the interbank market to stem the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline. \par \par \uc2  The rare announcement came after the won fell to a new low of 902.50, but its impact was only temporary. At the midday break, the won stood at 903.50. \par \par \uc2  Steve Marvin, head of research at Ssangyong Securities, said the won's dive was a response to a rash of corporate failures. \par \par \uc2  Corporate treasurers of firms whose imports feed South Korea's export industries have become jittery about monthly import settlements, he said. \par \par \uc2  "These guys have concluded the won is going to go down further. In a sense their worries are triggering early buying or stockpiling of dollars. They know they're going to need dollars. In a sense it's a self-fulfilling prophecy," Marvin said. \par \par \uc2  Graham Courtney, director of Asian economics at SBC Warburg in Tokyo, blamed South Korea's August trade deficit, which he forecast would be "very, very big," though exports recently have been increasing and imports have slowed. \par \par \uc2  "There is extra downward pressure on the won coming from what looks to be a particularly bad deficit," Courtney said. \par \par \uc2  Traders seem to disagree with recent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  statements that a won/dollar rate of 900 was reasonable, he said. \par \par \uc2  "Frankly, if the trade thinks the rate should be higher, why should the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  defend at 900? I think the Bank of Korea is now thinking it should move to a more defensible level, and fundamentally, 905-910 is much more defensible," he said. \par \par \uc2  The won is less susceptible than some \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to attack. Its daily movement is limited to a 2.25 percent trading band. \par \par \uc2  "Restrictions on won trading make it a less appetising target," Marvin said. "There's not that comfort zone of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  linked to the dollar," \par \par \uc2  The won has been depreciating against the U.S. unit for more than a year, falling 8.2 percent against the dollar last year, and more than six percent so far this year. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Bank of Korea \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market three times during the morning trading session. \par \par \uc2  It sold dollars at 923 won in six-month forward trade at about mid-morning, then stepped in again minutes later to sell dollars at 944 won in one-year forward trade, they said. \par \par \uc2  In late morning, the Bank of Korea entered the market for a third time and sold dollars at about 903.00 in spot trade. \par \par \uc2  The fourth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came in early afternoon when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars for 904 won in spot trading. \par \par \uc2  Near the close of trading, the Bank of Korea stepped in for the fifth time, selling dollars at 904.40-904.80 in spot trade. At the same time, the bank sold dollars at 907.50 in one-month forward and 916 in three-month forward trade. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt8p00thf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
