\par\par \b \uc2  Seoul stocks seen drifting lower on weak factors. \b0\par\par\uc2 203 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Aug 20 (Reuter) - Seoul stocks are expected to drift lower in slow trading on Wednesday as weakening fundamentals, including the won's fall against the dollar, continue to depress sentiment, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The composite stock index closed 6.00 points lower at 741.29 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "The index is likely to test the 720 level this week, given the deteriorating market factors," said Lee Moo-il of LG Securities. "But an intraday technical rebound is likely after six days of declines." High interest rates and falling fund inflows would also weigh on the market, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said blue chips would stay lethargic, while some theme-backed shares might thrive. \par \par \uc2  The won briefly hit a record low of 901.00 in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Tuesday before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Yields on three-year corporate bonds, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark interest rate closed at 12.32 percent, up from 12.25 percent on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Customer deposits with securities houses were now estimated nat 3.04 trillion won, compared with a yearly high of 3.6 trillion in mid-June. \par \par \uc2  --KIM MYONG-HWAN (822) 727 5645 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt8k00w0d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
