\par\par \b \uc2  Seoul stocks end lower on weak won, high rates. \b0\par\par\uc2 446 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Aug 19 (Reuter) - Stocks closed down for the sixth consecutive trading day on Tuesday as the weakening won and the rising interest rates hampered sentiment, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  "Fundamentals continued to worsen. A technical rebound is likely on Wednesday after a week-long consolidation, but overall sentiment shows no signs of recovery," said Kim Wan-hee of Dongsuh Securities. \par \par \uc2  The composite index closed 6.00 points lower at 741.29. \par \par \uc2  The won briefly hit the record low of 901.00 in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's repeated dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Yields on the three-year corporate bonds, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark interest rates, rose to 12.32 percent from 12.25 percent on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said the finance ministry's plan of additional sales abroad of shares in unlistng high-ranking Seoul officials, and authorities said an investigation has been going on to verify Hwang's testimonies. \par \par \uc2  Oh arrived in Pyongyang on Friday by train for "permanent residence", according to Pyongyang's official Korean Central News Agency's (KCNA). \par \par \uc2  South Korea's state television broadcast North Korean TV footage showing Oh stepping out of a train at Pyongyang railway station. \par \par \uc2  In an arrival statement, he hailed North Korean leader Kim Jong-il as a hero and slammed the South Korean government for pursuing policies hampering the reunification of the Korean peninsula divided at the end of World War Two. \par \par \uc2  The two Koreas have been locked in Cold War confrontation since the 1950-53 Korean war. \par \par \uc2  Volume totalled 31.61 million shares, compared with 28.98 million on Monday. Losers outpaced gainers 527 to 277 with 97 stocks unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Trading of primary blue chips were dull with buying limited to theme-backed shares. \par \par \uc2  KEPCO, which rose on Monday on bargain hunting, fell 200 won to 25,900. Samsung Electronics slid 100 won to 71,000 and POSCO fell 1,000 to 60,000 won. \par \par \uc2  Shares of Il-Yang Pharm rose 1,900 won to its daily upper-limit of 26,500 on a news report that the company had developed a raw material for semiconductors. \par \par \uc2  Il-Yang said the material, which will be used in production of one gigabit and four gigabit dynamic random access memory (DRAM) chips, would be supplied to Samsung Electronics, the world's leading memory chip maker. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said low levels of customer deposits with securities houses, now estimated at 3.08 trillion won against a yearly high of 3.6 trillion in mid-June, and rising loans for margin trading, valued at 3.3 trillion, was also eroding sentiment. \par \par \uc2  --KIM MYONG-HWAN (822) 727 5645 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt8j00uh0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
