\par\par \b \uc2  S. Korea  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Says Will Sell Dlr When Necessary \b0\par\par\uc2 157 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 1996\par 02:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL (AP-Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bank of Korea, will boldly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as the dollar continues to rise sharply against the won, a bank spokesman said Monday. \par \par \uc2  The spokesman, however, refused to say at what level the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  'We will sell dollars again when the dollar rises sharply,' the spokesman said. 'The dollar is expected to maintain strength this month on scarcity of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.' \par \par \uc2  The dollar has been shooting higher on weak economic conditions such as a snowballing current account deficit, the spokesman said. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won dipped lower early Monday, falling to around 844.25 won per dollar, as investors continued their dollar-buying fever from last week's record-breaking performance, traders said. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 16-12-96 \par \par \uc2  0159GMT \par \par \uc2  1063 1858 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011014dscg01nsd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
